//
// Phase C1 + D1 operator gate: addnorm, in both residual orderings.
//
// Weighted LayerNorm and a residual, with the weight as a runtime memref
// argument rather than baked into the MLIR. 64 rows, not 512: the builder
// requires one kernel call per tile, and that caps rows at what L1 holds
// across the 8-column herd. builders/addnorm.py records the measurement
// behind that constraint.
//
// TWO DIFFERENT FUNCTIONS, NOT TWO SHAPES OF ONE.
//   64x512          post-add, LayerNorm(x) * weight + residual
//   64x768_pre_add  pre-add,  LayerNorm(x + residual) * weight
//
// The second is what encoder_bert computes at both of its normalization points
// and it had never been dispatched before Phase D1: the kernel supported it
// from Phase A behind -DADDNORM_PRE_ADD, but no builder exposed the variant.
// It links a different object built from a different source (encoder.cc has no
// pre-add path at all), calls a different entry point, and is checked against
// its own reference. Both lines below must appear, in this order, because a
// pre-add build checked against the post-add oracle -- or the reverse -- is
// exactly the failure this pair exists to catch, and it is a failure that
// leaves the output the right shape.
//
// EACH FUNCTION ALSO RUNS AN OFFSET-REGIME SHAPE (doc 23 item 2's addnorm
// half): rows at a common offset large next to their spread (mean 8, sigma
// 0.25), residual identically zero so the row isolates the statistics. The
// one-pass bf16 statistics both kernels shipped with lose such rows' variance
// entirely -- measured 28170/32768 (post-add) and 43058/49152 (pre-add)
// outside tolerance, collapse boundary between |mean|/sigma 2 and 4 -- and
// the two-pass f32 statistics they carry now must not. These two rows are
// what pin that: a revert to one-pass statistics fails here rather than in a
// probe nobody runs. Their first gated hardware run (2026-08-11) measured
// mean_rel_L1 1.390e-3 / 1.409e-3 with atol_required 0.0 on both -- recorded
// in opcheck_specs.py; the atol figures stay the sibling rows' as
// never-binding slack.
//
// Runs opcheck.py on real NPU2 against an FP32 reference: np.isclose over the
// full output at rtol=1.6e-2, zero permitted mismatches. Not cosine similarity
// -- kernel_registry/README.md is explicit that a correlation gate is blind to
// a systematic per-element scale error.
//
// NPU serialization lives one layer down, not in the recipe below. Device
// access is locked on /tmp/npu.lock, which XRTRunner.run_test holds across
// xclbin load and dispatch (python/air/backend/xrt_runner.py:292) -- the same
// inode lit.cfg.py's NPU substitution and KernelCache take, so this interlocks
// with every other NPU job in the repository while the peano compiles still
// overlap. Taking the outer /tmp/mlir-air-npu.lock here instead would DEADLOCK:
// the gate command that invokes this suite already holds that inode, and BSD
// flock(2) treats a second open() of the same file as a foreign lock, so the
// nested acquire would block against its own parent until the timeout expires.
//
// Editing this header: lit scans the WHOLE file for its directives, so naming
// one in prose -- even inside a comment -- registers as a second directive and
// leaves the test UNRESOLVED. Describe them, never spell them out.
//
// REQUIRES: ryzen_ai_npu2, peano
//
// RUN: mkdir -p test_npu2_addnorm_peano
// RUN: cd test_npu2_addnorm_peano
// RUN: make -f %S/Makefile clean
// RUN: make -f %S/Makefile check-addnorm PEANO_INSTALL_DIR=%PEANO_INSTALL_DIR | FileCheck %s
//
// Both lines, not just the verdict: the first is XRTRunner's own, the second
// names the operator and shape, so a test wired to the wrong operator fails
// here rather than passing quietly.
// CHECK: PASS!
// CHECK: [opcheck] addnorm [64x512]: PASS
// The pre-add variant's own object is named in the log, so a run that silently
// fell back to encoder.o fails here rather than at the verdict.
// CHECK: Compiling addnorm_pre_add.o from addnorm_ffn.cc
// CHECK: PASS!
// CHECK: [opcheck] addnorm [64x768_pre_add]: PASS
// The offset rows, in declaration order: post-add first, then pre-add with
// the same object guard as above.
// CHECK: PASS!
// CHECK: [opcheck] addnorm [64x512_offset]: PASS
// CHECK: Compiling addnorm_pre_add.o from addnorm_ffn.cc
// CHECK: PASS!
// CHECK: [opcheck] addnorm [64x768_pre_add_offset]: PASS
